UNITS = [
dict(slug="01-modeling-identification", title="Modeling, identification and simulation", prerequisites="Differential equations, units, matrix least squares and elementary probability.",
lesson=r"""
## A model begins with a boundary

A controller changes a physical input; its software output is not automatically that input. Draw the vessel, inlet streams, outlet, sensor and valve before naming states. For a perfectly mixed tank with constant density and cross-sectional area $A$, conservation gives

$$A\dot h=q_H+q_L-q_o,\qquad \frac{d(Ahc)}{dt}=q_Hc_H+q_Lc_L-q_oc.$$

Expanding the derivative and substituting the volume balance cancels the outlet term in the concentration equation:

$$\dot c=\frac{q_H(c_H-c)+q_L(c_L-c)}{Ah}.$$

This cancellation does not mean the outlet is irrelevant: it changes $h$, which changes the residence time. Mixing is assumed instantaneous; density differences, reaction, evaporation and sensor dynamics are absent. A simulation approaching $h=0$ must stop or change its physical model rather than divide by a clipped volume silently.

## A common worked process

The exercises use hypothetical values $V=100$ L, total flow $q=10$ L/min and normalized feed concentrations $c_H=1$, $c_L=0$. A low-level flow system holds $q$ constant while $q_H=qu$ and $q_L=q(1-u)$, with $0\leq u\leq1$. Thus

$$\tau\dot c=u-c,\qquad \tau=V/q=10\ {\rm min}.$$

These are new teaching parameters, not a transcription of the archived Blending Tank files. At a constant input, $c^*=u^*$. In deviation variables, $G(s)=1/(10s+1)$. At a one-minute sampling period with a zero-order-held input,

$$c_{k+1}=ac_k+bu_k,\qquad a=e^{-0.1}=0.904837418,\quad b=1-a=0.095162582.$$

The analytical feed-composition problems use $d=d_{low}$ for the concentration of the low feed, giving an inlet mixture $u+(1-u)d_{low}$. The shared executable laboratory scenario instead uses an additive disturbance to the total inlet mixture, $u+d_{mix}$. Their conversion is $d_{mix}=(1-u)d_{low}$, so they are not interchangeable at fixed numerical values. Feedforward in the additive scenario compensates by $-d_{mix}$; the low-feed examples derive the nonlinear blend formula explicitly. Check the disturbance definition before comparing a worked answer with a laboratory run.

The exact discretization preserves the interval $[0,1]$ because the next concentration is a convex combination of the current concentration and the inlet blend. Forward Euler gives $a_E=1-T_s/\tau$; it is asymptotically stable for $0<T_s<2\tau$ but preserves this physical interval only for $0<T_s\leq\tau$.

## Identification is an experiment-design problem

Fit $y_{k+1}=ay_k+bu_k+e_{k+1}$ by least squares, $\hat\theta=(\Phi^T\Phi)^{-1}\Phi^TY$, only when the regressor matrix has full column rank. In software, use a QR/SVD least-squares solve rather than forming the inverse. A constant equilibrium record cannot identify both coefficients. Step tests help, but a bounded multilevel or persistently exciting signal gives a broader operating region.

Split complete trajectories chronologically into training, validation and final test sets. Fit scaling parameters on training data only. A one-step prediction uses the measured previous output; free simulation recursively uses its own prediction. Report both because a small one-step error can hide unstable recursive behavior. With noisy measured regressors, ordinary least squares can be biased; instrumental variables or a noise model may be needed.

## Evidence and interpretation

Check mass conservation, equilibria, convergence when the integration step is halved, and agreement with the analytical step response. Inspect residual autocorrelation and correlation with past inputs on an independent trajectory. Residual whiteness alone does not prove a correct physical model. Use the benchmark inventory to distinguish equation-based simulations from experimental datasets, and do not compare numerical scores obtained with different input protocols as if they were the same experiment.
""",
problems=[
("From vessel balances to an admissible simulation",r"""A tank initially contains 100 L at $c(0)=0.2$. The total inlet and outlet flows are 10 L/min and the high-concentration fraction becomes $u=0.7$. (a) Derive the concentration equation from total and solute balances, retaining the volume derivative until the end. (b) Obtain $c(t)$, its value at 10 min, and the time to reach 95% of its total change. (c) Explain what changes if the outlet is fixed at 8 L/min; derive the new volume and concentration equations and identify which transfer-function assumption fails.""",r"""(a) The total balance is $\dot V=q_H+q_L-q_o=0$. The solute balance is $V\dot c+c\dot V=q_H-q_oc$; substituting $q_H=10u$ and $q_o=10$ gives $10\dot c=u-c$. (b) $c(t)=0.7-0.5e^{-t/10}$, so $c(10)=0.5160603$. Solving $e^{-t/10}=0.05$ gives $t_{95}=29.9573$ min. The concentration remains between its initial and final values. (c) $V(t)=100+2t$ and $\dot c=10(0.7-c)/(100+2t)$. Separation gives $c(t)=0.7-0.5[100/(100+2t)]^5$. The response slows as the vessel fills; the constant residence-time, time-invariant transfer function no longer applies. A finite tank also needs an overflow limit."""),
("A sampled model that respects concentration bounds",r"""Use the same tank with a held input. (a) Derive the exact sampled model for an arbitrary $T_s$ and prove interval invariance. (b) Compare its pole with Euler at $T_s=1$, 15 and 25 min. (c) A numerical model produces a negative concentration after a valid inlet change. Decide which evidence would distinguish an integration artifact from a wrong balance or inconsistent initial condition.""",r"""(a) Integration yields $c_{k+1}=e^{-T_s/10}c_k+(1-e^{-T_s/10})u_k$. Both coefficients are nonnegative and sum to one, proving invariance for admissible $c_k,u_k$. (b) Exact poles are approximately 0.90484, 0.22313 and 0.08208. Euler poles are 0.9, -0.5 and -1.5. At 15 min Euler is stable but can leave the physical interval; at 25 min it is unstable. (c) Re-run with successively smaller steps, check the analytical solution for a constant input and audit units and state initialization. Convergence to a physically impossible result points beyond time-step error. Do not conceal the failure by clipping the state, since clipping creates or destroys material."""),
("Identify a process without leaking the answer",r"""Noiseless input-output samples are $(c_k,u_k,c_{k+1})=(0.2,0.8,0.26)$ and $(0.26,0.1,0.244)$. (a) Estimate $a,b$ and identify the time constant if these are exact one-minute samples. (b) Show why equilibrium-only observations do not uniquely determine both parameters. (c) Design independent training, validation and test trajectories for a noisy experiment and state which model choices may use each split.""",r"""(a) Solving $0.2a+0.8b=0.26$ and $0.26a+0.1b=0.244$ gives $a=0.9$, $b=0.1$. The exact continuous time constant would be $-1/\ln(0.9)=9.49122$ min; interpreting this as the earlier Euler discretization instead gives 10 min. The discretization convention matters. (b) At equilibrium $u_k=c_k$ and $c_{k+1}=c_k$, so only $a+b=1$ is identified: the columns of $\Phi$ coincide. (c) Use different bounded multilevel sequences and different initial concentrations; reserve the last complete sequence for final reporting. Fit coefficients and scaling on training data, choose model order/regularization using validation data, then evaluate the frozen model once on test data. Report free-run and one-step errors separately."""),
("Disturbance linearization and sensor dynamics",r"""Let the low-concentration feed carry an unknown normalized concentration $d$. Its balance is $10\dot c=u+(1-u)d-c$. A concentration sensor obeys $2\dot y=c-y$. (a) Linearize around $(u,d,c)=(0.4,0,0.4)$ and obtain both measured-output transfer functions. (b) Find the steady concentration after $d$ rises to 0.1 with fixed $u=0.4$. (c) Explain why fitting a single first-order model to $u\mapsto y$ can distort controller tuning.""",r"""(a) The derivatives are $\partial f/\partial c=-0.1$, $\partial f/\partial u=0.1$, $\partial f/\partial d=0.06$. Therefore $Y/U=1/[(10s+1)(2s+1)]$ and $Y/D=0.6/[(10s+1)(2s+1)]$ in deviation variables. (b) The exact steady value is $0.4+0.6(0.1)=0.46$; the local prediction happens to be exact for this disturbance with fixed $u$. (c) A single lag may absorb the sensor's lag into an effective residence time or delay. An aggressively tuned controller then acts on a slower measurement than the plant-only design assumes. Identify sensor dynamics independently where possible, and report whether the model output is physical concentration or the measured signal."""),
("Choose an identification claim that the evidence supports",r"""A model gives test errors $e=[0.1,-0.1,0.2,-0.2]$ on $y=[0,1,0,1]$. (a) Compute SSE, MSE, RMSE, maximum absolute error and $R^2$. (b) Give a case where a small one-step error would coexist with poor free simulation. (c) Propose two checks that would make a comparison against a model from Kroll and Schulte meaningful, and one conclusion these four samples cannot support.""",r"""(a) SSE is 0.10, MSE 0.025, RMSE $\sqrt{0.025}=0.158114$, and maximum absolute error 0.2. The target mean is 0.5 and the sum of squared deviations is 1, hence $R^2=0.90$. (b) A recursively simulated autoregressive model with a pole slightly above one can make small corrections when measured outputs are supplied, yet drift when fed its own outputs. (c) Match the paper's plant variant, parameters, sampling, input sequence, initial state and noise convention; keep a common independent test record and metric normalization. Four samples do not establish generalization, robustness or physical validity. The article uses MAE for maximum absolute error; write the metric out to avoid confusing it with mean absolute error."""),
]),
dict(slug="02-feedback-imc", title="Feedback, stability and IMC tuning", prerequisites="Unit 01; Laplace transforms, characteristic polynomials and negative feedback.",
lesson=r"""
## Separate the plant, controller and actuator

For the hypothetical tank $P(s)=1/(\tau s+1)$, define the tracking error $e=r-y$ and a PI command $v=K_pe+I$, with $\dot I=K_ie$. The applied blend is $u=\operatorname{sat}_{[0,1]}(v)$. A control diagram that omits this saturation cannot explain windup. For a PID controller, differentiate a filtered measurement rather than an abrupt reference when derivative kick is undesirable; for example use $-K_ds/(1+T_fs)$ on $y$.

With ideal measurement, no saturation and PI feedback, the closed-loop characteristic polynomial is

$$\tau s^2+(1+K_p)s+K_i.$$

The second-order continuous model is asymptotically stable when $\tau>0$, $1+K_p>0$ and $K_i>0$. This result does not cover delays, sampled implementation or saturating nonlinear dynamics. The final-value theorem also requires a stable closed loop before it can justify zero constant-reference error.

## Derive an IMC design rather than memorize gains

For the stable minimum-phase model $\hat P=1/(\tau s+1)$, choose $Q=(\tau s+1)/(\lambda s+1)$. Converting the internal-model structure to ordinary feedback gives

$$C=\frac{Q}{1-\hat P Q}=\frac{\tau s+1}{\lambda s},\qquad K_p=\frac{\tau}{\lambda},\quad K_i=\frac1\lambda.$$

Nominal pole-zero cancellation makes $Y/R=1/(\lambda s+1)$, but the canceled pole still matters to internal dynamics and mismatch. With $\tau=10$ min and $\lambda=5$ min, $K_p=2$ and $K_i=0.2$ per minute. For a first-order-plus-dead-time approximation, the commonly used IMC-PI rule $K_p=\tau/[K(\lambda+\theta)]$, $T_i=\tau$ trades speed for delay tolerance; it is an approximation, not exact cancellation of a delay.

## Implement the controller that was analyzed

One explicit digital update is $v_k=K_pe_k+I_k$, $u_k=\operatorname{sat}(v_k)$, then $I_{k+1}=I_k+T_s[K_ie_k+K_{aw}(u_k-v_k)]$. Here $K_{aw}$ has units of inverse time because $I$ has actuator units. Conditional integration is another option: freeze integration when saturation and the error would push farther into the limit. Keep update order and derivative state explicit in MATLAB, Octave and Python.

The local discrete closed-loop matrix for the unsaturated explicit update and zero reference is

$$M=\begin{bmatrix}a-bK_p&b\\-T_sK_i&1\end{bmatrix}.$$

Its eigenvalues, not continuous poles alone, determine sampled stability. A reference beyond the attainable concentration range has no feasible equilibrium, however stable the local model is.

## Compare outcomes on the same experiment

Use identical initial state, reference steps, feed disturbances and actuator limits. Record tracking error, settling definition, total variation $\sum|u_k-u_{k-1}|$, saturation time and any violation. A smoother input may be valuable even if its integral absolute error is larger. Disturbance rejection and sensor-noise amplification are distinct design goals; one appealing tracking plot cannot demonstrate both.
""",
problems=[
("An IMC reference design with a physical actuator",r"""The tank operates initially at $c=u=0.4$ and the reference changes to 0.6. (a) Derive PI gains for $\lambda=5$ min and the nominal reference response. (b) Compute the immediate actuator command using the equilibrium integral state. (c) Repeat for a reference of 1.2 and explain which part of the nominal calculation ceases to predict the real response.""",r"""(a) $K_p=10/5=2$ and $K_i=1/5=0.2$ per minute. The ideal deviation response is $0.2(1-e^{-t/5})$. (b) At the original equilibrium, $I=0.4$; immediately after the step, $v=2(0.2)+0.4=0.8$, within the bounds. (c) The larger error is 0.8, giving $v=2.0$, but $u$ can only be 1. Concentration approaches at most 1. The linear closed-loop transfer function assumes $u=v$ and cannot predict saturation or the accumulating integral state. The target itself is infeasible, so anti-windup can improve recovery but cannot make it attainable."""),
("A stable continuous design with an unstable software choice",r"""For $K_p=2$, $K_i=0.2$ per minute, use the exact held-input plant and the explicit integral update in the lesson. (a) Derive the closed-loop matrix. (b) Evaluate its eigenvalues for $T_s=1$ min. (c) Test $T_s=10$ min and explain why the continuous-time stability proof is insufficient.""",r"""(a) With zero reference, $e_k=-c_k$ and $c_{k+1}=(a-bK_p)c_k+bI_k$, while $I_{k+1}=I_k-T_sK_ic_k$. This gives the stated matrix. (b) For $a=0.9048374$, the trace is 1.7145123 and determinant 0.7335448; eigenvalues are approximately $0.89390714$ and $0.82060511$, inside the unit circle. (c) For $a=e^{-1}$, the matrix has trace about 0.1036383 and determinant $e^{-1}=0.3678794$. The poles are a complex pair of magnitude $\sqrt{e^{-1}}=0.606531$, so this particular sample remains stable, although response quality differs. This is a deliberate check against assuming coarse sampling always destabilizes. At $T_s=20$ min the determinant is $2-e^{-2}=1.864665>1$, so at least one pole is outside the unit circle. Derive and test the implemented update instead of guessing."""),
("Recover from windup",r"""At one sample $I=0.9$, $e=0.2$, $K_p=2$, $K_i=0.2$, $T_s=1$ min. (a) Calculate requested and applied commands. (b) Calculate the next integral state without anti-windup and with $K_{aw}=0.5$ per minute. (c) Explain why back-calculation should use actual actuator feedback when the valve differs from the software saturation.""",r"""(a) $v=0.9+0.4=1.3$ and $u=1$. (b) Without correction, $I^+=0.9+0.04=0.94$. With back-calculation, $I^+=0.9+0.04+0.5(1-1.3)=0.79$. The correction removes the portion of the integral state responsible for an unrealizable command. (c) A stuck valve, rate limit or failed low-level flow loop can make the actual blend differ from the clipped software request. Tracking actual actuator feedback aligns the internal controller state with what the plant receives. Sensor delay in that feedback must itself be considered. Excessively fast back-calculation can create undesirable sampled transients."""),
("Delay uncertainty and a robustness budget",r"""An identified model is $P(s)=e^{-2s}/(10s+1)$. (a) Apply the stated approximate IMC-PI rule for $\lambda=5$ min. (b) Compare gains to the delay-free design. (c) Propose a reproducible experiment with delay ranging from 1 to 4 min and explain which result would disqualify the fast tuning even if nominal IAE is smaller.""",r"""(a) $K_p=10/(5+2)=10/7$ and $T_i=10$ min, hence $K_i=1/7$ per minute. (b) The delay-free gains are 2 and 0.2, so both new gains are $5/7$ of those values. The lower gain accounts approximately for the missing phase information caused by delay. (c) Use the same bounded reference and disturbance trajectories at each delay, with a delay buffer that represents fractional samples explicitly. Record stability, peak error, constraint violations, IAE and actuator variation. Sustained oscillation, growing error, or violation of a declared operating limit in an allowed delay case disqualifies a candidate even if its nominal tracking integral is lower. A finite grid is evidence for those cases, not a proof for every delay."""),
("Choose metrics before choosing the winner",r"""Controller A has absolute errors $[0.2,0.1,0.05,0]$ and applied inputs $[0.8,0.3,0.7,0.5]$. Controller B has errors $[0.2,0.12,0.06,0.02]$ and inputs $[0.6,0.58,0.55,0.52]$. Assume one-minute equal-duration intervals and an initial input of 0.4. (a) Compute rectangle-rule IAE and total input variation. (b) Compare $J=\mathrm{IAE}+0.1\,\mathrm{TV}(u)$. (c) Explain what a fair report must disclose about this scalar score.""",r"""(a) A has IAE 0.35 and variation $0.4+0.5+0.4+0.2=1.5$. B has IAE 0.40 and variation $0.2+0.02+0.03+0.03=0.28$. (b) $J_A=0.50$ and $J_B=0.428$, so B wins under this explicitly chosen cost although A tracks more tightly by IAE. (c) State the sample interval, quadrature convention, input normalization, initial input and weight units. Changing the weight can reverse the ranking; neither controller is universally superior. Treat hard safety/operating constraints separately rather than allowing a favorable average score to compensate for violations."""),
]),
dict(slug="03-control-structures", title="Multiloop, multivariable, feedforward and cascade control", prerequisites="Units 01-02; matrix inversion and local transfer functions.",
lesson=r"""
## Match architecture to the physical disturbance path

Feedback acts after an output error is visible. Feedforward uses a measured disturbance before that error develops. For $10\dot c=u+(1-u)d-c$, holding the equilibrium at $r$ requires

$$u_{ff}=\frac{r-d}{1-d},\qquad d\ne1.$$

For $0\leq d<1$, this is feasible only when $d\leq r\leq1$. Feedback can trim errors in $d$, mixing and actuator calibration; it cannot repair an impossible target. The linear cancellation formula is $F(s)=-G_d(s)/G_u(s)$. A nonminimum-phase zero or unfavorable delay in $G_u$ can make the ideal inverse unstable or noncausal, so practical feedforward often uses a filtered approximation.

## Cascade closes a faster measured loop

Suppose a valve command $v$ produces the actual blend $u$ through $\tau_v\dot u=v-u+w$, where $w$ is a valve/load disturbance. A fast inner controller regulates measured $u$ to an outer command $u_{sp}$; the outer concentration controller then sees the closed inner dynamics. With inner proportional gain $K_f$ and the feedforward equilibrium command $v=u_{sp}+K_f(u_{sp}-u)$, the inner pole is $-(1+K_f)/\tau_v$. A practical cascade needs an inner sensor, useful bandwidth separation and consistent actuator limits. A fast model is not evidence that an actual noisy valve can move arbitrarily quickly.

## Interactions require a joint view

For a local steady two-input/two-output gain matrix $K$, the relative gain array is $\Lambda=K\circ(K^{-1})^T$, where $\circ$ denotes elementwise multiplication. For

$$K=\begin{bmatrix}1&0.6\\0.4&1\end{bmatrix},\qquad \Lambda=\begin{bmatrix}1.315789&-0.315789\\-0.315789&1.315789\end{bmatrix}.$$

Diagonal pairing avoids negative relative gains here. RGA is a local steady-state diagnostic, not a stability proof: dynamics, delays and operating-point changes still matter. Static decoupling $u=K^{-1}v$ removes steady interaction for the nominal model, but can demand infeasible or rapidly changing physical inputs. If saturation occurs, the assumed decoupling is lost.

## Compare architectures under matched conditions

Start with independently tuned PI loops, then add measured-disturbance feedforward, cascade or decoupling for a stated reason. Keep the total available measurements explicit; a design with an additional disturbance sensor has an information advantage. Introduce one disturbance at a time, then simultaneous changes and model mismatch. Report output-by-output errors as well as a combined score. In Simulink, keep the physical plant, measurement path and controller subsystems separate so their responsibilities remain visible.
""",
problems=[
("A feed composition disturbance",r"""The concentration target is 0.5. The low feed changes from $d=0$ to 0.2 while total flow remains constant. (a) Calculate the new blend fraction required for zero steady error. (b) Compare exact nonlinear feedforward with the linearization around $u=0.5,d=0$. (c) Determine what occurs if $d$ rises to 0.7 and the target is unchanged.""",r"""(a) $u=(0.5-0.2)/(1-0.2)=0.375$. (b) The local relation is $\delta c=\delta u+0.5\delta d$, giving $u_{lin}=0.5-0.5(0.2)=0.4$. Its actual equilibrium is $0.4+0.6(0.2)=0.52$, so feedback must remove 0.02 concentration error. (c) Even $u=0$ gives concentration 0.7; any high-feed addition increases it. The target is infeasible. The supervisor should flag the condition or change the admissible target rather than continue integrating an error that the actuator cannot remove."""),
("When perfect feedforward cannot be built",r"""Suppose $G_u=e^{-3s}/(10s+1)$ and $G_d=0.6e^{-s}/(10s+1)$. (a) Derive the ideal cancellation transfer function. (b) Explain the implementation obstacle. (c) Propose an implementable architecture and an experiment that separates feedforward benefit from ordinary feedback correction.""",r"""(a) $F=-G_d/G_u=-0.6e^{2s}$. (b) This needs the disturbance two minutes into the future; the actuator path is slower than the disturbance path. A causal controller cannot supply an unknown future value. (c) Use upstream sensing that provides at least two minutes of genuine transport preview if available; otherwise use a causal gain/filter and accept residual transient error, with feedback trimming. Compare feedback alone and feedback plus the feasible feedforward on the same recorded disturbance and initial state. Account for sensor location, latency and noise. Do not model an artificial negative delay and describe the resulting cancellation as implementable."""),
("Design the inner flow loop before the outer loop",r"""A blend actuator has $\tau_v=2$ min and the vessel has $\tau=10$ min. Use $v=u_{sp}+K_f(u_{sp}-u)$. (a) Choose $K_f$ for an inner time constant of 0.5 min. (b) Derive the transfer functions from $u_{sp}$ and $w$ to $u$. (c) Discuss what a valve limit and sensor noise can invalidate in the desired bandwidth separation.""",r"""(a) $2/(1+K_f)=0.5$, so $K_f=3$. (b) From $2\dot u=(1+K_f)u_{sp}-(1+K_f)u+w$, $U/U_{sp}=4/(2s+4)$ and $U/W=1/(2s+4)$. The steady load sensitivity is reduced from 1 to 1/4. (c) Saturation prevents the requested initial response, and a noisy inner sensor is multiplied by the feedback gain. Rate limits add dynamics that the first-order design omits. Before tuning the outer loop around the 0.5-minute response, verify the inner loop over its actual working range, including setpoint bounds and disturbance amplitudes."""),
("Pairing and decoupling a coupled process",r"""Use $K=\begin{bmatrix}1&0.6\\0.4&1\end{bmatrix}$. (a) Calculate its inverse and relative gain array. (b) Find the steady input change required for output change $[0.2,0]^T$. (c) If the available input deviations are each limited to $[-0.1,0.1]$, explain why independent output targets cannot be assumed achievable.""",r"""(a) $K^{-1}=\frac1{0.76}\begin{bmatrix}1&-0.6\\-0.4&1\end{bmatrix}$ and the RGA has diagonal $1/0.76=1.315789$ and off-diagonal $-0.24/0.76=-0.315789$. (b) The required change is $[0.263158,-0.105263]^T$. (c) Both demands exceed their respective limits in magnitude, so the target is infeasible under these bounds. Saturating the inverse output destroys nominal cancellation and creates error in both outputs. A constrained optimization can select an attainable compromise, with output weights reflecting declared priorities."""),
("Select a structure with a fair information budget",r"""A concentration loop is affected by slow inlet-composition drift and rapid supply-pressure disturbances at its valve. Available upgrades are an upstream composition sensor and an inner flow sensor. (a) Map each sensor to the disturbance it can address. (b) Design a three-case comparison that attributes improvement to architecture rather than extra actuator range. (c) State why a static RGA from one operating point cannot settle the full design choice.""",r"""(a) The composition sensor enables feedforward for changes in the entering material. The flow sensor enables a cascade loop that rejects supply disturbances before concentration changes appreciably. (b) Compare feedback only, feedback plus measured composition feedforward, and that architecture plus inner flow regulation. Hold plant parameters, final actuator bounds, reference schedule, disturbance realizations and initial conditions fixed; disclose the added sensors and their noise/latency. Report concentration error and valve movement separately. (c) RGA describes local steady gains; it does not represent disturbance location, measurement timing, dynamic interaction, noise amplification or actuator constraints. Repeat local analyses across operating points and validate the chosen dynamic structure."""),
]),
dict(slug="04-model-predictive-control", title="Constrained model predictive control", prerequisites="Units 01-03; quadratic forms, least squares and constrained optimization.",
lesson=r"""
## Predict several steps, apply only the first action

For $x_{k+1}=Ax_k+Bu_k$, $y_k=Cx_k$, assemble predicted outputs as $Y=Fx_k+GU$, where $U=[u_k^T,\ldots,u_{k+N-1}^T]^T$. The first block rows are $CA$ in $F$ and $CB$ in $G$; the second are $CA^2$ and $[CAB,CB]$. Use a consistent convention: these predictions begin at $k+1$.

A useful objective is

$$J=(Y-R)^TQ(Y-R)+(DU-d_0)^TR_u(DU-d_0),$$

where $DU-d_0$ stacks input increments and its first element is $u_k-u_{k-1}$. Bounds on $U$, input increments and predicted outputs become linear inequalities. Penalizing input magnitude instead of increments solves a different problem, especially around nonzero operating points. The Hessian is positive definite when the regularization supplies strictly positive curvature in every free input direction.

At each sample, estimate the state, solve the optimization, apply the first action, and repeat. Use a shorter control horizon by holding the last planned move constant; write this mapping explicitly rather than silently changing the prediction length. Log solver status, time and fallback use. A failed solver must not be treated as a valid optimum.

## One step exposes the tradeoff

For $c^+=ac+bu$ and $J=(c^+-r)^2+\rho(u-u_{old})^2$, the unconstrained optimum is

$$u^*=\frac{b(r-ac)+\rho u_{old}}{b^2+\rho}.$$

With only scalar interval constraints, project this value onto their intersection. This is exact for the scalar one-step problem; independently clipping a general coupled multistep solution is not equivalent to solving its constrained quadratic program.

## Constraints and offset have different causes

If the target lies beyond the attainable set, no horizon removes that incompatibility. Soften selected output targets with nonnegative slack and an explicit penalty when the application permits it; keep actuator bounds hard. For persistent model mismatch, augment a constant disturbance state and estimate it, or add another justified offset-free mechanism. A large prediction horizon alone does not guarantee zero steady error.

## Stability is a design property to establish

Finite-horizon optimization and decreasing simulated error do not automatically imply closed-loop stability. Classical sufficient conditions involve suitable terminal cost, terminal invariant constraints and a feasible terminal controller. The teaching implementations demonstrate finite-horizon behavior under their declared experiments; do not infer a global nonlinear stability theorem. For multivariable processes, inspect individual output constraints and input allocation, not just the sum of squared errors.
""",
problems=[
("Build the prediction matrices by hand",r"""For $c^+=ac+bu$, use $a=0.9,b=0.1$ and a prediction horizon of three. (a) Write $F$ and $G$ for $Y=[c_{k+1},c_{k+2},c_{k+3}]^T$. (b) Predict the sequence for $c_k=0.2$ and planned inputs $[0.5,0.7,0.4]^T$. (c) Explain which action a receding-horizon controller actually sends to the plant and when the other actions may change.""",r"""(a) $F=[0.9,0.81,0.729]^T$ and $G=\begin{bmatrix}0.1&0&0\\0.09&0.1&0\\0.081&0.09&0.1\end{bmatrix}$. (b) Multiplication, or recursive substitution, gives $[0.23,0.277,0.2893]^T$. (c) Only 0.5 is applied now. At the next sample, the state estimate and reference forecast may differ, so the optimization is repeated and the previous 0.7/0.4 plans are not commitments. All language implementations must use the same initial state and timing to obtain matching predictions."""),
("Solve a constrained one-step controller",r"""Use $a=0.9,b=0.1$, $c=0.2$, $r=0.8$, $u_{old}=0.4$ and $\rho=0.01$. (a) Derive and calculate the unconstrained optimum. (b) Enforce $0\leq u\leq1$ and $|u-u_{old}|\leq0.1$. (c) Compute the next concentration and explain why clipping the reference would solve a different problem.""",r"""(a) Differentiating the objective gives $2b(ac+bu-r)+2\rho(u-u_{old})=0$. Thus $u^*=[0.1(0.8-0.18)+0.01(0.4)]/0.02=3.3$. (b) The intersection of amplitude and rate bounds is $[0.3,0.5]$, so the exact constrained optimum is 0.5. (c) $c^+=0.9(0.2)+0.1(0.5)=0.23$. The target remains a desired future output even though it cannot be reached in one step; clipping it to an immediately reachable value changes the performance objective and future plan. In this scalar convex problem projection is exact, but that property does not extend to arbitrary coupled QPs."""),
("Input increments and operating-point offsets",r"""A controller operates at $u=0.6$. (a) Write the difference matrix and initial-value vector for three planned absolute inputs. (b) Compare the penalties on $U=[0.6,0.6,0.6]^T$ when penalizing $U^TU$ and $(DU-d_0)^T(DU-d_0)$. (c) Explain the effect of using absolute inputs in a model identified in deviations.""",r"""(a) $D=\begin{bmatrix}1&0&0\\-1&1&0\\0&-1&1\end{bmatrix}$ and $d_0=[0.6,0,0]^T$. (b) The magnitude penalty is $3(0.6)^2=1.08$; the increment penalty is zero. The former encourages lower absolute actuation, while the latter only discourages movement. (c) A deviation model expects $\delta u=u-u^*$ and predicts $\delta y=y-y^*$. Feeding absolute $u$ introduces a systematic operating-point error and may lead the optimizer to inappropriate actions. Store and test the offsets explicitly, including state initialization, reference translation and actuator bounds in deviation coordinates."""),
("A multivariable feasibility calculation",r"""At steady state $y=Ku$ with $K$ from Unit 03 and $0\leq u_i\leq1$. Target $y=[1.4,0.2]^T$. (a) Calculate the unconstrained input. (b) Prove that this target is infeasible. (c) Formulate a constrained steady target optimizer with output weights 4 and 1 and explain how it can support a dynamic MPC.""",r"""(a) Apply the inverse from Unit 03:

$$u=\frac{1}{0.76}\begin{bmatrix}1.4-0.12\\-0.56+0.2\end{bmatrix}=\begin{bmatrix}1.684211\\-0.473684\end{bmatrix}.$$

(b) Because $K$ is nonsingular this is the unique input producing the target; both components violate bounds. (c) Solve $\min_{0\leq u\leq1}(Ku-r)^T\operatorname{diag}(4,1)(Ku-r)$. Its optimum is a feasible compromise, not an exact realization of $r$. A steady target layer can supply consistent equilibrium inputs/outputs to the dynamic regulator, avoiding an impossible terminal goal. The 4:1 weights express priority; report the resulting errors in both outputs."""),
("A solver failure is an operating event",r"""A constrained MPC normally finishes in 0.02 s but occasionally exceeds a 0.1 s sampling deadline. (a) Design a causal fallback and identify what state it must retain. (b) Explain how disturbance-state augmentation can address steady mismatch but not a missed deadline. (c) Specify a validation record that prevents an apparently good output plot from hiding solver failures.""",r"""(a) One conservative fallback is to hold the previous feasible input after applying declared rate and amplitude limits, while recording the event. Retain the actual applied input and the latest trustworthy state estimate. A separately validated backup controller is another option; transfer its integral state consistently. (b) A constant-disturbance estimator can correct bias in model predictions, improving offset behavior, but it does not provide computing time or a guaranteed feasible optimization result. (c) Log solve status, elapsed time, objective, maximum constraint residual, input actually applied and fallback flag at every sample. Report deadline misses and their effects separately, including worst-case intervals. Neither a successful solver status nor a short nominal trace proves recursive feasibility for untested conditions."""),
]),
dict(slug="05-expert-fuzzy-control", title="Knowledge-based supervision and fuzzy control", prerequisites="Units 01-03; sets, piecewise functions and numerical integration.",
lesson=r"""
## Supervisory rules express decisions and evidence

A rule such as IF the concentration residual is large AND the flow sensor agrees with its command THEN suspect a concentration-path fault represents a diagnostic hypothesis. It does not prove a failed sensor. Store the measured evidence, persistence interval, action and reset condition separately. Use priorities for conflicting rules: an invalid measurement should inhibit a normal optimization request. Hysteresis and dwell time prevent repeated switching near a threshold. A low concentration alone cannot distinguish an inlet change, valve fault or concentration-sensor bias.

## Fuzzy membership is a degree of compatibility

On a normalized error domain $[-1,1]$, define negative, zero and positive memberships by $\mu_N(e)=\max(0,-e)$, $\mu_Z(e)=1-|e|$ and $\mu_P(e)=\max(0,e)$, after explicitly limiting $e$ to the domain. They sum to one. These numbers are not probabilities unless a separate probabilistic interpretation is established. Input scaling determines which physical errors activate each rule.

For a two-input controller using error and error change, a rule might be IF error is positive AND error change is positive THEN increase the blend strongly. Specify whether AND is minimum or product. Mamdani inference clips or scales an output membership function for every firing rule, aggregates the results, and often defuzzifies with the centroid:

$$u_f=\frac{\int z\,\mu_{out}(z)\,dz}{\int\mu_{out}(z)\,dz}.$$

For zero-order Sugeno inference, singleton consequents $z_i$ give $u_f=(\sum_i w_i z_i)/(\sum_i w_i)$. This generally differs from the centroid of overlapping Mamdani sets. If no rule fires, use a documented fallback; adding an arbitrary epsilon to the denominator does not define a meaningful inference result.

## Fuzzy control can act at several levels

A fuzzy controller may directly choose the command, produce its increment, or schedule PI/PID gains. If $u_k=u_{k-1}+K_u u_f$, then $K_u$ and the sampling interval determine how quickly the actuator moves. A fuzzy PID schedule changes $K_p,K_i,K_d$ with operating conditions; integrate with the current gain convention consistently and retain anti-windup. A stable PI at every fixed gain setting does not by itself prove stability during arbitrary gain switching.

For the tank, positive concentration error should generally increase the high-feed fraction. The rule surface must respect this known sign. Examine its monotonicity, continuity and saturated behavior before closing the loop. Compare against the same PI baseline with identical sensors and limits. Display both the membership functions and the resulting control surface; a list of linguistic rules alone is insufficient for reproduction.
""",
problems=[
("Diagnose a symptom without overclaiming a fault",r"""During a 20-minute experiment the concentration residual becomes positive, the commanded blend is 0.5, and measured blend remains 0.3. (a) Construct a rule that flags a flow-tracking problem after three consecutive samples. (b) Add a reset condition with hysteresis. (c) Explain which alternative hypotheses remain and what additional measurement would separate them.""",r"""(a) For one-minute samples, define $r_u=u_{cmd}-u_{meas}$ and flag when $r_u>0.1$ for three consecutive valid samples; attach the evidence and sample times. With residual 0.2 this condition is met after the third sample. Name the state 'flow-tracking discrepancy', not 'valve definitely stuck'. (b) Reset only after $|r_u|<0.05$ for five valid samples; invalid sensor data should enter a distinct unknown state rather than count as successful tracking. (c) Valve limitation, supply-pressure loss, calibration error or a faulty flow sensor can all produce this record. Independent valve-position/supply-pressure measurement or a redundant flow check helps distinguish them. Concentration alone adds delayed, model-dependent evidence."""),
("A reproducible Sugeno control action",r"""Use the three memberships from the lesson with normalized error $e=0.3$. Single-input zero-order consequents are $z_N=-1,z_Z=0,z_P=1$. (a) Calculate every membership and the Sugeno output. (b) If this gives an input increment with $K_u=0.2$ and previous input 0.96, determine the requested and applied actions. (c) Explain why changing the error normalization changes the physical controller.""",r"""(a) Memberships are $[0,0.7,0.3]$, so the weighted output is 0.3. (b) The requested increment is 0.06; the requested command is 1.02 and the applied blend is 1.0. Record the difference for any internal integrator or anti-windup mechanism. (c) If $e=e_{physical}/E$, reducing $E$ produces a larger normalized error for the same physical condition and activates more extreme rules. Thus normalization acts like a gain and must be part of the controller specification. The normalized domain and saturation policy also determine behavior outside the trained/design range."""),
("Mamdani centroid is not singleton averaging",r"""Two disjoint triangular output sets have centers -0.5 and 0.5, each base width 1 and height 1. Their supports are $[-1,0]$ and $[0,1]$. Minimum implication clips them at heights 0.8 and 0.2; aggregation is maximum. (a) Calculate each clipped area. (b) Calculate the centroid of the aggregate. (c) Compare with a Sugeno system using the centers as singleton consequents and the same firing strengths.""",r"""(a) For a symmetric triangle of base width 1, clipping at height $h$ leaves area $h-h^2/2$. The areas are 0.48 and 0.18. Each clipped set retains its center by symmetry. (b) The supports do not overlap except at a zero-membership endpoint, so the total first moment is $-0.5(0.48)+0.5(0.18)=-0.15$ and the area is 0.66. The centroid is $-0.2272727$. (c) The Sugeno result is $[-0.5(0.8)+0.5(0.2)]/(0.8+0.2)=-0.3$. The difference comes from the nonlinear dependence of clipped area on firing strength. A program that returns -0.3 for this Mamdani construction has implemented a different inference system."""),
("A fuzzy gain schedule with an explicit implementation",r"""Let $K_p(e)=1+2\min(|e|/0.2,1)$ and $K_i=K_p/10$. (a) Calculate the gains at errors 0, 0.1 and 0.3. (b) Write a sampled PI update with saturation and back-calculation using these gains. (c) State why testing the three fixed-gain linear models is useful but insufficient to validate the gain-scheduled loop.""",r"""(a) The proportional gains are 1, 2 and 3; the integral gains are 0.1, 0.2 and 0.3 per minute. (b) At sample $k$, evaluate $K_{p,k},K_{i,k}$ from the current error, calculate $v_k=K_{p,k}e_k+I_k$, apply $u_k=\operatorname{sat}_{[0,1]}v_k$, and update $I_{k+1}=I_k+T_s[K_{i,k}e_k+K_{aw}(u_k-v_k)]$. This integral state is already in actuator units. (c) Fixed-gain tests reveal local behavior and numerical errors, but the actual system is nonlinear and time-varying. Rapid scheduling, noise, delays and saturation may produce behaviors absent in frozen models. Test transitions and perturbations throughout the operating region; a formal global claim requires additional analysis."""),
("Validate the rule surface before a closed-loop comparison",r"""A two-input fuzzy controller uses concentration error and its sampled difference. (a) Specify a grid-based test that checks correct sign, boundedness and coverage. (b) Explain why a symmetric rule table need not produce an odd control surface. (c) Design an ablation comparison between PI, fuzzy PI and fuzzy gain scheduling.""",r"""(a) Evaluate a dense grid including corners and every membership breakpoint; require a finite output within declared limits and nonzero aggregate firing. Along zero error change, check that positive error does not command a decrease in high-feed fraction under the chosen sign convention. Examine neighboring values for unintended jumps. (b) Asymmetric input/output membership widths, asymmetric normalization or non-symmetric saturation can break odd symmetry even if linguistic labels appear symmetric. (c) Keep reference, disturbance, noise, sample rate, initial state and actuator limits identical. Compare PI, an incremental fuzzy controller, and a PI with only fuzzy gain scheduling. Report tuning effort and all gains/rules. This separates the effect of inference from the effect of merely changing nominal gain or actuator smoothing."""),
]),
dict(slug="06-neural-identification-control", title="Neural modeling and control of dynamic systems", prerequisites="Units 01, 02 and 04; differentiation, gradients, least squares and separation of training, validation and test data.",
lesson=r"""
## Predict a dynamic relation, not a shuffled collection of rows

A nonlinear autoregressive model with external input uses past observations and applied inputs to predict the next output. One causal choice is

$$\phi_k=[y_k,y_{k-1},u_k,u_{k-1}]^T,\qquad \hat y_{k+1}=f_\theta(\phi_k).$$

The regressor timing must be causal. Randomly shuffling heavily overlapping windows before splitting can place nearly identical trajectories in both training and testing. Split trajectories first, then construct lagged windows inside each partition. Save scaling parameters, random seed, architecture, weights and the complete data protocol.

## Four network families address different tasks

A perceptron computes a binary decision from $\operatorname{sign}(w^Tx+b)$ and updates the separating hyperplane on classification mistakes. It is a useful diagnostic classifier for linearly separable conditions, not a continuous process simulator. An MLP composes affine maps and nonlinearities; for one hidden layer, $h=\tanh(W_1\phi+b_1)$ and $\hat y=W_2h+b_2$. Backpropagation differentiates the loss through both layers. Regularization, early stopping and independent validation limit overfitting but do not certify extrapolation.

An RBF network uses local responses $\psi_j(\phi)=\exp[-\|\phi-\mu_j\|^2/(2\sigma_j^2)]$ and a linear output. For fixed centers/widths, ridge regression determines output weights from $(\Psi^T\Psi+\alpha I)w=\Psi^TY$. Poorly placed centers can leave important operating regions unsupported. A self-organizing map (SOM) updates a best-matching unit and its neighbors, $m_j^+=m_j+\eta h_{j,j^*}(x-m_j)$. It organizes operating regimes without supervised target values; a small map distance is not a fault label or a probability.

## Accuracy is a property of the validation experiment

Report one-step and recursive simulation errors, operating-range coverage, residual structure and behavior under changed initial conditions. Normalize errors with a declared physical scale. Preserve the physical concentration interval and compare against the linear identification baseline: a larger network is useful only if it answers a limitation the baseline actually has. Bootstrap or repeated-seed comparisons should resample whole independent runs where temporal correlation matters.

## Use a learned model in control with explicit safeguards

A neural predictor can replace a prediction model inside MPC, or a differentiable inverse can propose an input. For a locally monotone model, a bounded Newton step is $u^+=u-\gamma(\hat y^+(u)-r)/(\partial\hat y^+/\partial u)$. A small or wrong-sign derivative makes this unreliable. Enforce actuator limits, restrict the search to supported inputs, test the candidate against a known model/constraint layer, and retain a validated fallback. Direct inverse learning can be ill-posed when different inputs produce the same output or when unobserved states matter. Good training error does not establish closed-loop stability.
""",
problems=[
("A perceptron for a simple supervisory condition",r"""Inputs are binary indicators $x_1$ (high residual) and $x_2$ (flow mismatch); the desired alarm is their logical OR. (a) Give weights and bias implementing the desired truth table with output 1 when $w^Tx+b\geq0$. (b) Starting from $w=[0,0]$, $b=0$ and learning rate 1, calculate the update after the sample $x=[0,0],t=0$, then after $x=[1,0],t=1$. (c) Explain why this classifier does not localize a specific physical fault.""",r"""(a) $w=[1,1]$ and $b=-0.5$ classify only $[0,0]$ as zero. (b) The initial prediction for $[0,0]$ is 1 because of the specified tie rule. With $\Delta w=(t-\hat t)x$ and $\Delta b=t-\hat t$, the new state is $w=[0,0],b=-1$. The next sample is incorrectly predicted zero, so $w=[1,0],b=0$. (c) OR identifies a combined alarm condition, not a unique cause. Several faults and ordinary operating changes can generate either indicator. Validate labels, persistence and class imbalance, and preserve the distinction between classification performance and causal diagnosis."""),
("Differentiate a minimal neural predictor",r"""For one scalar regressor $x=0.5$, let $h=\tanh(w_1x)$, $\hat y=w_2h$, $w_1=1,w_2=0.8$, target $y=0.4$ and loss $L=(\hat y-y)^2/2$. (a) Compute the prediction. (b) Derive both weight gradients. (c) State how a finite-difference check and a separate validation trajectory catch different errors.""",r"""(a) $h=\tanh(0.5)=0.4621172$ and $\hat y=0.3696937$. (b) With residual $e=-0.0303063$, $\partial L/\partial w_2=eh\approx-0.0140050$. The chain rule gives $\partial L/\partial w_1=ew_2(1-h^2)x\approx-0.0095337$. Gradient descent subtracts these derivatives, so both weights initially increase. (c) Centered finite differences compare code derivatives with the implemented loss and detect chain-rule/indexing errors. An independent trajectory tests whether learning generalizes beyond the fitted samples. A correct gradient can still optimize an overfitted or causally invalid model."""),
("Fit and interpret a two-center RBF",r"""Use centers 0 and 1, width $\sigma=1$, no bias term, and training pairs $(x,y)=(0,0),(1,1)$. (a) Write the design matrix. (b) Solve for the two exact interpolation weights. (c) Explain what predictions far outside $[0,1]$ reveal about this architecture and why regularization changes interpolation.""",r"""(a) With $r=e^{-1/2}$, $\Psi=\begin{bmatrix}1&r\\r&1\end{bmatrix}$. (b) Solving gives $w_1=-r/(1-r^2)\approx-0.9595174$ and $w_2=1/(1-r^2)\approx1.5819767$. The interpolating values follow directly by substitution. (c) Both radial basis functions approach zero far from their centers, so the prediction approaches zero regardless of the process physics. This is unsupported extrapolation, not evidence that the real output vanishes. Ridge regularization shrinks the weights and usually sacrifices exact interpolation to reduce variance and ill-conditioning; choose its strength using independent validation."""),
("A SOM organizes operating modes",r"""A two-unit SOM has prototypes $m_1=[0.2,0.3]^T$ and $m_2=[0.8,0.7]^T$. The normalized observation is $x=[0.3,0.4]^T$. Use learning rate 0.5, neighborhood weight 1 for the winner and 0.2 for the other unit. (a) Find the winner. (b) Calculate both updates. (c) Explain how training-data scaling and a subsequent fault label affect interpretation.""",r"""(a) Squared distances are 0.02 and 0.34, so unit 1 wins. (b) $m_1^+=[0.2,0.3]+0.5[0.1,0.1]=[0.25,0.35]$. The second unit moves by $0.5(0.2)([-0.5,-0.3])$, giving $m_2^+=[0.75,0.67]$. (c) Euclidean distances depend on feature scales, so estimate normalization on training data and keep it fixed. A cluster of high residuals is not automatically a known fault; attach labels only using independently documented operating/fault evidence. A SOM can reveal a regime shift while leaving its physical cause unresolved."""),
("Put a neural prediction inside a bounded control decision",r"""A local learned model predicts $\hat c^+=0.9c+0.1u+0.02u^2$. At $c=0.4$, current $u=0.4$ and desired next concentration 0.5, use a Newton inverse step with $\gamma=1$. (a) Calculate the prediction, derivative and candidate input. (b) Enforce $0\leq u\leq1$ and $|u-u_{old}|\leq0.1$. (c) Give two reasons to retain a fallback even if this training model fits well.""",r"""(a) The prediction is $0.36+0.04+0.0032=0.4032$, and the derivative is $0.1+0.04(0.4)=0.116$. The Newton candidate is $0.4-(0.4032-0.5)/0.116=1.234483$. (b) The feasible interval is $[0.3,0.5]$, so the bounded candidate is 0.5; its prediction is 0.415. The target is not immediately reachable within the rate limit. (c) The model derivative may be unreliable outside its supported region, and plant mismatch or unobserved dynamics may invalidate the prediction. Repeated bounded steps are not a closed-loop stability proof. Compare on independent trajectories and use a tested controller when confidence or numerical conditions fail."""),
]),
dict(slug="07-evolutionary-tuning", title="Evolutionary optimization and controller tuning", prerequisites="Units 02, 04 and 06; random sampling, objective functions and constrained experiment design.",
lesson=r"""
## Encode a controller and an experiment together

A genetic algorithm searches a population of candidate parameters through selection, crossover and mutation. For PI tuning the chromosome may encode $(K_p,K_i)$; for multivariable MPC it may encode prediction horizon and positive input/output weights. Bounds and units are part of the representation. A chromosome is not a controller until the decoding, simulation protocol, constraints and cost are defined.

For a $b$-bit unsigned integer $q$, a parameter in $[l,h]$ is $\theta=l+(h-l)q/(2^b-1)$. Binary encoding creates quantization and bit-level neighborhoods that may differ strongly from physical distance. Real encoding avoids that quantization but still needs a documented crossover and mutation distribution. Blend crossover $\theta_c=\alpha\theta_1+(1-\alpha)\theta_2$ stays inside the parental interval when $0\leq\alpha\leq1$; Gaussian mutation may leave bounds, so repair or rejection must be specified.

## Reward what matters and reject invalid trials

One cost is $J=\mathrm{IAE}+\beta\sum_k|u_k-u_{k-1}|+\gamma\sum_k\epsilon_k^2$, where $\epsilon$ measures permitted soft violations. Normalize terms or state their units before choosing weights. Hard physical limits should be enforced or treated as candidate rejection, not hidden by an average penalty. Terminate unstable simulations using declared state/finite-value thresholds and give them a consistently poor fitness.

Tournament selection needs only an ordering, whereas fitness-proportional selection needs nonnegative appropriately scaled fitness. Elitism preserves the best observed valid candidate; it does not establish a global optimum. Count simulation evaluations, not just generations, when comparing computational budgets. Include a grid, random search or established IMC tuning baseline so improvement can be attributed to the search method rather than additional evaluation effort.

## Separate tuning scenarios from final evaluation

Optimize against a fixed family of training trajectories, select algorithm settings using validation scenarios, then freeze the design before testing unseen disturbances and plant mismatch. Repeated seeds reveal stochastic search variability; report the distribution and all failed runs, not only the best seed. For uncertain models, use a declared robust objective such as the maximum cost across a finite scenario set; this is robustness to that tested set, not a proof for all uncertainty.

For multivariable MPC, chromosomes must retain positive weights and integer horizons. Changing a weight changes both performance and the meaning of a reported objective; compare final controllers using a common external evaluation metric. Include solve-time and feasibility effects because a long horizon that misses the deadline is not an operational improvement.
""",
problems=[
("Binary resolution has a physical cost",r"""Encode $K_p\in[0,4]$ with four bits. (a) Decode chromosome 1001 and compute its resolution. (b) Find the nearest representable value to $K_p=2$. (c) Determine the minimum number of bits needed for resolution no greater than 0.02 and discuss the effect on search-space size.""",r"""(a) Binary 1001 is integer 9, hence $K_p=4(9)/15=2.4$. Resolution is $4/15=0.2666667$. (b) Integers 7 and 8 give 1.8666667 and 2.1333333, equally far from 2. There is no exact code for 2 with this endpoint mapping. (c) Require $4/(2^b-1)\leq0.02$, or $2^b\geq201$, so $b=8$. A single parameter now has 256 codes instead of 16; two such parameters have 65,536 combinations. More resolution increases possible candidates but does not by itself improve the algorithm's ability to find a useful region."""),
("Selection, crossover and mutation",r"""Two real chromosomes are $p_1=[2,0.2]$ and $p_2=[1,0.1]$. (a) Perform blend crossover with $\alpha=0.25$. (b) Apply mutation $[0.2,-0.15]$ and repair into bounds $K_p\in[0,4]$, $K_i\in[0.02,0.5]$ by projection. (c) Explain how a two-member tournament chooses among costs 0.6 and 0.4 and why recording the random seed is insufficient without the full evaluation protocol.""",r"""(a) $p_c=0.25p_1+0.75p_2=[1.25,0.125]$. (b) Mutation gives $[1.45,-0.025]$; projection gives $[1.45,0.02]$. The repair creates a pileup at the lower bound if many mutations cross it, which can affect diversity. (c) For minimization the cost-0.4 member wins. A seed reproduces pseudo-random choices only with the same generator, ordering and software behavior. Initial states, plant parameters, sampling, constraints, termination thresholds and cost definitions are also needed to reproduce fitness and selection."""),
("A fitness function that does not hide violations",r"""Candidate A has IAE 0.35, input variation 1.5 and no violations. Candidate B has IAE 0.30, variation 0.4 and one violation of a declared hard concentration limit. (a) Rank them under the soft cost $J=\mathrm{IAE}+0.1\,\mathrm{TV}$. (b) Rank them when the declared hard constraint is enforced. (c) Explain how to treat a simulation that becomes nonfinite rather than completing the horizon.""",r"""(a) The scalar costs are 0.50 and 0.34, so B appears better if the violation is ignored. (b) B is infeasible and must be rejected or ranked behind every feasible candidate under a feasibility-first policy; A is preferred. A hard limit cannot be traded away merely because tracking is good. (c) Detect the nonfinite state, terminate the trial, label it invalid and assign the predetermined worst fitness. Do not average only the successful prefix, because an unstable controller would then appear cheap by ending early. Preserve the failure count and cause in optimization results."""),
("Tune MPC parameters without changing the ruler",r"""A chromosome contains horizon $N$, output weight $q$ and move weight $r_u$. Bounds are $N\in\{3,\ldots,20\}$, $q\in[0.1,10]$, $r_u\in[0.001,1]$. (a) Propose a valid decoding for real genes. (b) Explain why multiplying both weights by 10 does not change an exact unconstrained optimizer. (c) Define an external fitness that still permits meaningful comparison among these chromosomes.""",r"""(a) Round a bounded horizon gene to the nearest allowed integer and decode weight genes logarithmically, for example $q=10^{z_q}$ with $z_q\in[-1,1]$ and $r_u=10^{z_r}$ with $z_r\in[-3,0]$. Log encoding allocates resolution across ratios. (b) A positive common scaling multiplies the entire objective by a constant and leaves its minimizer unchanged, although solver numerical tolerances can be affected. (c) Use a fixed external tracking/variation/constraint metric on the same scenarios, independent of the internal MPC weights, and include deadline misses. Otherwise candidates could lower their reported internal objective simply by shrinking both weights without changing any control behavior."""),
("Assess a stochastic tuning claim",r"""Two search methods each use population 20 for 15 evaluated generations, including their initial population. Method A reports only its best seed; B reports ten seeds. (a) Calculate the nominal evaluation budget per seed. (b) Propose a matched experiment and final test. (c) Explain why a lower best training cost does not prove superior generalization or a global optimum.""",r"""(a) The nominal budget is $20\times15=300$ evaluations if every generation evaluates 20 candidates and caching/elitism do not reduce the count. Report actual calls as well. (b) Run both methods for the same number of independent seeds with matched total simulation budgets and identical training scenarios. Choose any algorithm settings on validation scenarios, freeze the selected design, then evaluate a separate family of disturbances, delays and plant parameters. Report median, spread, failures and evaluation time. (c) The best seed is a selection-biased statistic, and repeated training queries can overfit the scenario family. A finite heuristic search does not prove a global optimum. A defensible claim is an observed improvement under the specified budgets and independent test protocol."""),
]),
dict(slug="08-integrated-validation", title="Integration, benchmark evidence and technical communication", prerequisites="Units 01-07; interpretation of numerical experiments and model limitations.",
lesson=r"""
## A comparison is a controlled experiment

Start by stating the plant, operating range, measured variables, actuator limits and decision question. Establish the physical and linear identification baselines before adding neural or fuzzy components. Freeze a shared experiment contract: sample interval, numerical solver and tolerance, initial state, reference, disturbances, noise realization, controller initialization and failure policy. Keep synthetic outputs distinct from measured records. A simulation-based controller study is evidence about its declared model and scenarios.

Use the supplied integrated syllabus as a non-official study framework. The three proposed practical blocks become one connected project: identify and tune a baseline; compare alternative structures and MPC; then add an intelligent method and test its incremental value. Do not give every method a different experiment merely because one plot looks better.

## Match the article's model identity before its score

Kroll and Schulte's article provides a benchmark survey, not a single self-contained software package. Its sections include artificial SISO and MIMO identification models; data repositories; an inverted pendulum; throttle; pneumatic actuation; hydrostatic transmission; three tanks; a bioreactor; a chemical CSTR; a binary distillation column; the ALSTOM gasifier; wind-turbine models; and the Tennessee Eastman process. Some examples specify equations; others require external data, maps, parameter choices or full reference simulators.

Record the section, equation/model variant, source version, parameters, units and any closure added by the implementation. An educational closure can illuminate a mechanism, but its results do not reproduce an external industrial benchmark. Missing physical functions must remain a visible dependency rather than be replaced by an arbitrary function with the original benchmark's name. The repository's benchmark inventory is the authority for implemented versus externally dependent cases.

## Evaluate performance and limitations jointly

Prediction metrics include maximum absolute error, SSE, RMSE, VAF and $R^2$. VAF uses residual variance while $R^2$ uses residual squared magnitude, so a constant bias can leave VAF deceptively high. For constant targets, denominator-based metrics may be undefined; report absolute errors rather than inventing a finite score. Control metrics include IAE/ISE, peak error, settling under a stated band/dwell rule, applied effort, total variation, saturation and violations. Analyze trajectories as well as aggregates.

## Communicate a reviewable conclusion

A technical report should connect requirements, derivation, implementation, experiment, result and limitation. Include a physical equipment diagram, a control architecture diagram and axes with units. Give enough code and configuration to reproduce every reported value. State which language/runtime was actually executed. An unrun MATLAB or Simulink port must not be presented as experimentally verified merely because Python passed.

Present improvements as conditional results: for this model, this information set and these tests, the observed tradeoff was favorable. Explain failures and unsupported extrapolation. A useful next decision may be to collect a missing measurement or validate a plant parameter, rather than introduce another algorithm.
""",
problems=[
("Design an integrated Blending Tank study",r"""Prepare a comparison of IMC-PI, feedforward PI, constrained MPC and one fuzzy or neural method on the hypothetical tank. (a) Define a common reference/disturbance protocol with a training period and a separate final test. (b) State the measurements each controller receives and how to disclose additional information. (c) Specify six reported outcomes and the minimum evidence required to reproduce them.""",r"""(a) One valid protocol starts all runs at $c=u=0.4$, uses one-minute sampling and a 120-minute horizon, with reference 0.4 until 20 min, 0.6 until 70 min, then 0.3. Tune on this schedule with a low-feed disturbance of 0.1 at 50 min; freeze designs, then test a new schedule with step times 15/65 min and disturbance 0.15 at 45 min, plus a declared changed residence time. Keep the exact schedule in configuration. (b) All controllers receive concentration and the previous applied input. Feedforward additionally receives measured inlet composition; this information advantage must be explicit. Use the same sensor noise and delay for shared channels. (c) Report IAE, peak error, settling rule/result, input variation, saturation duration and violations/fallbacks. Save parameters, seeds, initial states, numerical settings, applied input and output trajectories, plus the executable command producing the report. These are designed experiments, not claimed outcomes."""),
("A biased model can have perfect VAF",r"""Measured targets are $y=[0,1,2,3]$ and a model predicts $\hat y=y+0.5$. (a) Compute RMSE, VAF and $R^2$ using population variances and the standard definitions. (b) Explain why the metrics disagree. (c) Propose an additional validation check and explain what to report for a constant-output test record.""",r"""(a) Every residual is -0.5, so RMSE is 0.5. Residual variance is zero and target variance is 1.25, giving VAF 100%. Residual SSE is 1 while the target centered sum of squares is 5, so $R^2=0.8$. (b) VAF removes the residual mean; it measures unexplained variation while ignoring a constant bias. $R^2$ retains that bias in SSE. (c) Report mean residual and maximum absolute error, and inspect residual versus operating point. For constant target data the variance denominator is zero: VAF and standard $R^2$ are undefined. Report that status and absolute metrics instead of silently setting either score to 0 or 100%."""),
("Distinguish reproduction from a teaching model",r"""Consider three submissions: A implements the article's specified artificial recurrence with matching input/initial conditions; B simulates a wind rotor with an invented constant torque coefficient; C runs an official Tennessee Eastman implementation with a documented disturbance schedule. (a) Classify what each can support. (b) List metadata needed before comparing A or C with published scores. (c) Explain how B can still be useful without overstating its identity.""",r"""(a) A is an equation-based reproduction candidate, subject to matching conventions and numerical checks. B is an educational wind-rotor approximation, not a reproduction of the full wind benchmark. C is a reference-simulator experiment, subject to version, controller and protocol matching. (b) Record model/source version, parameters, units, initial state, sampling, inputs/disturbances, noise, data split, controller information, metric formula and failure policy. Match these before comparing scores. (c) B can demonstrate inertia, aerodynamic/mechanical power balance and sensitivity to a chosen coefficient. Name and document the approximation, identify the missing map and restrict conclusions to its simplified dynamics. Do not use the benchmark name alone as evidence of comparability."""),
("Cross-language agreement has several levels",r"""Python and Octave simulations differ by $10^{-7}$ in concentration but by 0.02 in a threshold-based settling time. MATLAB/Simulink has not yet run. (a) Explain a plausible cause of the settling difference. (b) Propose numerical and behavioral cross-language checks. (c) Write an accurate validation statement.""",r"""(a) A trajectory near the settling-band boundary can cross it at adjacent samples after tiny floating-point differences; a threshold statistic is discontinuous even when trajectories agree closely. Different sample indexing or dwell conventions can also cause larger differences and should be checked. (b) Compare equilibria, one-step transitions, dimensions, units, applied bounds, maximum absolute trajectory error and identical sampling/metric logic. Include a case comfortably away from threshold boundaries and a case intentionally near them. (c) An accurate statement is: Python and Octave passed the specified trajectory and constraint checks; a boundary-sensitive settling statistic differed under the recorded convention; MATLAB/Simulink execution remains unverified. Do not turn source availability into a runtime claim."""),
("Make a defensible recommendation from mixed results",r"""In a fixed synthetic test family, a tuned controller reduces median IAE by 12% but increases input variation by 40%, misses two deadlines in 1,000 samples, and was tested at only one residence time. (a) State a supported conclusion. (b) Identify which additional experiments address generalization, timing and actuator wear. (c) Give a report outline that makes both the benefit and limitations inspectable.""",r"""(a) The controller improved median tracking error on the declared synthetic cases, with higher actuator movement and observed deadline misses. The evidence does not establish overall superiority or robustness to residence-time variation. (b) Test held-out reference/disturbance schedules and a justified range of plant parameters; measure solve time under realistic computational load and exercise the fallback; evaluate valve rate limits and a relevant wear/energy model rather than treating variation alone as a complete wear prediction. (c) Organize the report as requirements and physical model; baseline/alternative derivations; common experiment contract; tracking and effort trajectories; per-run metrics and failures; sensitivity/uncertainty results; execution record; limitations and the next decision. The recommendation should reflect the actual priorities and admissible deadline policy."""),
]),
]

